<TopicPage slug="subjects/history/advanced/historiography-debates-and-schools">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Historiography is <strong>the history of history-writing</strong>: how each generation's
    assumptions, politics and sources shape what it sees in the past. It is the field's
    self-awareness layer — and the reason "the history of" any topic is a contested, living
    argument rather than a settled record. Reading the debates teaches you what every history is
    doing, including this atlas's.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="four schools, one discipline — each still active" widget="equationStep" config={histioCfg} />

  <h2>Famous debates as case studies</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>The origins of World War I:</strong> war-guilt (Versailles) → Fischer's German drive for power (1960s) → Clark's "sleepwalkers" sharing responsibility (2012). Each generation found the war it needed; the archives grew; the question survived all of them.</li>
    <li><strong>The fall of Rome:</strong> invasion-and-catastrophe (Gibbon's decay narrative) vs transformation (Peter Brown's "Late Antiquity") — the same evidence, differently framed, with archaeology (climate, plague data) shifting the balance.</li>
    <li><strong>Quantification wars:</strong> cliometrics and statistical history vs cultural close-reading. Both added; the mature field uses both — and knows which claims each kind of evidence can support.</li>
    <li><strong>Why this matters to you:</strong> every textbook, including this atlas, embodies choices. Historiography is the skill of spotting them — in any field's history, including science's own.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Braudel, The Mediterranean; Jenkins, Rethinking History; Eley, A Crooked Line.</p>
</TopicPage>
